course: dictionary
lesson: 5778
title: Lustre: a shine with meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lustre is a noun. It means a soft, shining glow on a surface, like the shine on pearls, silk or healthy hair. In American English it is spelt luster. Lustre can also mean a special quality or fame that makes something seem bright and admired.
explain: «Geologists identify minerals by hardness, colour, lustre (shine), streak and how they break.» The brackets give you a clue: lustre means shine. In science, lustre describes how a mineral reflects light, such as metallic, glassy, pearly or dull.
explain: «The event lost its lustre» means it became less exciting. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Lustre means a soft, shining glow on a surface.
fact: Geologists use lustre to identify minerals: gold has a metallic lustre, and quartz has a glassy one.
fact: Pearls have a pearly lustre, and some minerals have a dull lustre.
fact: «Lose its lustre» means to become less exciting or less special.
fact: «Add lustre to» means to make something more admired or impressive.
exercise: «Geologists identify minerals by hardness, colour, lustre (shine), streak and how they break.» What does lustre mean here? | Size | Weight | *Shine
exercise: Gold has a metallic lustre and quartz has a glassy one. What is lustre in this sentence? | *The way a mineral reflects light | The hardness of a mineral | The colour of a mineral's powder
exercise: In «lustre (shine)», what do the brackets tell you? | *They give the meaning of lustre | The word is a spelling mistake | The word is a question
test: «Her silk dress had a soft lustre in the candlelight.» What does lustre mean here? | A loud noise | A heavy weight | *A soft, shining glow
test: «The old trophy had lost its lustre.» What does this suggest? | *It was no longer exciting or admired | It was made of gold | It had been polished that morning
exam: Which best describes lustre? | A loud, sudden noise | A type of rock | *A shine on a surface, or a quality that makes something admired
